window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][3]={"university_biology_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Chemical bonds and aqueous solutions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> School chemistry: atoms, ions and\namount of substance; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>: quantitative\nrelationships.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish bonding types and\nquantitatively explain concentration, dilution and hydration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Cytoplasm is not a pure liquid. It contains dissolved ions, small\nmolecules, proteins and larger structures. Their interactions determine\nwhich reactions and transport processes are possible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"bonds-and-interactions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bonds and interactions<\/h3>\n<p>Covalent bonds involve shared electron density between atoms. Unequal\nelectronegativity gives rise to a polar bond. Ions form through electron\nloss or gain; opposite charges attract. A hydrogen bond is a directional\ninteraction between a suitable donor and acceptor, not an additional\ncovalent O–H bond. Dispersion forces also act between nonpolar groups.\nMany individually weak contacts can collectively stabilize protein\nstructure.<\/p>\n<p>Water has a dipole moment because of its bent geometry and polar\nbonds. In a cation’s hydration shell, water preferentially points its\noxygen side toward the ion; near anions, its hydrogen side predominates.\nHydrophobic molecular groups tend to associate in water because under\nsuitable conditions this lowers the free energy of the entire system.\nThis does not imply formation of a new chemical bond.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"amount-of-substance-and-concentration\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Amount of substance and\nconcentration<\/h3>\n<p>Amount concentration is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=n/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith amount\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin mol and volume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin L. Mass is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=nM<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis molar mass. For dilution without solute loss,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_1V_1=c_2V_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOsmolarity accounts for the number of dissolved particles per volume;\ncharge, activity and membrane permeability are considered separately\nlater.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_012_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Water dipoles around sodium and chloride ions, schematic, not to scale.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Water dipoles around sodium and chloride\nions, schematic, not to scale.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Water dipoles around sodium and chloride ions, schematic,\nnot to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional calculation considers 10 mL of 0.20 mol/L NaCl solution\nand a final volume of 50 mL. The amount is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0020<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\cdot0.010=0.0020<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmol. Final concentration is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0020<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.050<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0020/0.050=0.040<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmol/L. Ideal complete dissociation gives approximately 0.080 osmol/L.\nThis does not determine tonicity across a particular cell membrane.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the hydration shell of Na+ oriented differently from that of\nCl−?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the amount in 25 mL of a 0.10 mol/L solution.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to concentration if volume doubles without solute\nloss?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The partially negative O side preferentially faces Na+; the\npartially positive H side faces Cl−.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.025<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0025<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.025\\cdot0.10=0.0025<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmol.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It halves because the amount of solute remains constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Concentration is not an exact substitute for activity in nonideal\nsolutions. Hydration shells are dynamic rather than rigid. Osmolarity\nand tonicity are not equivalent.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Bonding, solvation and concentration connect molecular structure to\naqueous system properties. M3-L2 examines their energetic direction.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-2-water\">OpenStax –\nBiology 2e, 2.2 Water<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Polarity, hydrogen bonding, solvent\nproperties · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – Reaction equilibrium and thermodynamics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>: concentration and\ninteractions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish Gibbs energy,\nreaction quotient, equilibrium and kinetic barriers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A cell maintains its organization through exchange of matter and\nenergy with its surroundings. Thermodynamics explains possible changes\nof state, but not their speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"energy-entropy-and-gibbs-energy\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy, entropy and Gibbs\nenergy<\/h3>\n<p>The first law accounts for energy: with work on the system taken as\npositive,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=q+w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second law requires a positive entropy change of system plus\nsurroundings for spontaneous processes. Local order can arise if the\noverall balance satisfies this condition. At constant temperature and\npressure, Gibbs energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=H-TS<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides a criterion:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T\\Delta S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNegative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfavours the direction considered; positive values favour the\nreverse.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"chemical-equilibrium\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Chemical equilibrium<\/h3>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>⇌<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\rightleftharpoons B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=a_B/a_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith dimensionless activities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMore generally, activities enter with stoichiometric exponents. The\nrelationship is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G=\\Delta_r G^\\circ+RT\\ln Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G^\\circ=-RT\\ln K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&lt;K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe forward direction is thermodynamically favoured. At dynamic\nequilibrium, forward and reverse reactions occur at equal rates. An\nenzyme lowers the activation barrier without changing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor reaction Gibbs energy under identical conditions. Coupling to an\nexergonic reaction can enable an otherwise unfavourable overall\nprocess.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_013_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic Gibbs energy along the reaction coordinate; identical endpoints with and without a catalyst.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic Gibbs energy along the reaction\ncoordinate; identical endpoints with and without a\ncatalyst.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Schematic Gibbs energy along the reaction coordinate;\nidentical endpoints with and without a catalyst.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat 298 K in a model. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.314<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=8.314\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>G<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5.70<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G^\\circ=-5.70<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkJ/mol. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>11.41<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G=RT\\ln(Q/K)=-11.41<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkJ/mol. The forward direction is favoured, but the reaction time cannot\nbe calculated without knowing the barrier.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which direction is favoured when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a catalyst change\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two coupled steps have reaction Gibbs energies of +8 and −15 kJ/mol.\nWhat is the total?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reverse, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q&gt;K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>G<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta_r G&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; under unchanged thermodynamic conditions it accelerates approach\nto equilibrium.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">−7 kJ/mol, provided the two steps are actually coupled.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Standard and actual states must be distinguished. Real cells often\noperate in nonequilibrium steady states. A favourable total energy\nbalance does not prove mechanistic coupling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Thermodynamics gives direction and equilibrium; kinetics gives time\nbehaviour. The next lesson examines reactive molecular groups.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Open systems; first\nand second laws · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-2-equilibrium-constants\">OpenStax\n– Chemistry 2e, 13.2 Equilibrium Constants<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Equilibrium constant\nand reaction quotient · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Organic functional groups<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>: covalent bonds and\npolarity.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Recognize functional groups and\npredict their contributions to reactivity, charge and solubility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Sugars, fatty acids and amino acids possess carbon skeletons but\ndiffer in the groups attached to them. These determine many possible\nreactions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>Carbon can form four bonds and produce chains, rings and branched\nskeletons. Double bonds restrict rotation. Constitutional isomers have\nthe same molecular formula but different connectivity; stereoisomers\ndiffer in spatial arrangement with unchanged connectivity. Chirality is\nimportant in enzyme recognition of substrates.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group<\/th>\n<th>Representation<\/th>\n<th>Typical significance<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hydroxyl<\/td>\n<td>R–OH<\/td>\n<td>polar, hydrogen bonding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carbonyl<\/td>\n<td>C=O<\/td>\n<td>electrophilic carbon; aldehyde or ketone<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carboxyl<\/td>\n<td>R–COOH<\/td>\n<td>weak acid; often R–COO− at neutral pH<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amino<\/td>\n<td>R–NH₂<\/td>\n<td>can be protonated<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thiol<\/td>\n<td>R–SH<\/td>\n<td>redox reactions, disulfide bonds<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Phosphate ester<\/td>\n<td>R–O–PO(OH)₂<\/td>\n<td>often negatively charged in water<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>R denotes a variable organic residue. Neighbouring groups influence\nacidity and reactivity. Esters can formally be described as forming from\na carboxylic acid and alcohol with loss of water; amide bonds connect\ncarbonyl carbon and nitrogen. Cells often form such bonds through\nactivated intermediates rather than simply mixing reactants in\nwater.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_014_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Six functional groups in simplified structural formulas.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Six functional groups in simplified\nstructural formulas.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Six functional groups in simplified structural\nformulas.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Lactic acid has the structure CH₃–CH(OH)–COOH. It contains hydroxyl\nand carboxyl groups and a stereogenic carbon atom. At pH 7 its carboxyl\ngroup is predominantly carboxylate. Ethanol CH₃–CH₂–OH has no carboxyl\ngroup. Comparing properties solely by carbon atom count would miss this\ndifference.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which group distinguishes acetic acid from ethanol with respect to\nacidity?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do constitutional isomers and enantiomers differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which groups of an amino acid may be ionized in water?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Acetic acid’s carboxyl group, which can release a proton.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Constitutional isomers differ in connectivity; enantiomers are\nnonsuperimposable mirror images with the same connectivity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Carboxyl and amino groups; depending on the side chain, additional\ngroups.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A functional group does not determine properties in isolation:\nenvironment, pH, charge distribution and molecular geometry act\ntogether. The drawings are simplified structural formulas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Organic groups explain molecular interactions and underpin\nbiochemistry. M3-L4 turns to physical transport models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-3-carbon\">OpenStax –\nBiology 2e, 2.3 Carbon<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Functional groups and isomers · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Optics, mechanics and transport processes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>: functions; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>: aqueous solutions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate optical resolution,\nmechanical forces and the influence of geometric parameters on\ntransport.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Microscopy reveals spatial structures; transport models explain how\nsubstances reach them. Both are subject to physical limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"optics-and-mechanics\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optics and mechanics<\/h3>\n<p>Magnification describes the image-to-object size ratio; resolution is\nthe ability to distinguish adjacent details. For a diffraction-limited\noptical system, the Rayleigh criterion gives the approximate lateral\nseparation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.61<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.61\\lambda/NA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NA=n\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis numerical aperture and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes vacuum wavelength. More magnification alone does not improve\nresolution. Fluorescence adds contrast through selective light emission\nfrom appropriate labels.<\/p>\n<p>Force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand pressure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=F/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare different quantities. Pressure differences drive volume flow. For\nsteady laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a rigid cylindrical tube,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\pi r^4\\Delta p/(8\\eta l)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand dynamic viscosity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRadius is therefore particularly influential. Diffusion instead arises\nfrom thermal motion; in simple Fickian diffusion the net flux follows\nthe concentration gradient:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=-D\\,dc/dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA characteristic one-dimensional diffusion distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2Dt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_015_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Relative Poiseuille volume flow at constant pressure difference, length and viscosity.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Relative Poiseuille volume flow at\nconstant pressure difference, length and viscosity.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Relative Poiseuille volume flow at constant pressure\ndifference, length and viscosity.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=550<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnm and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NA=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>268<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx268<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnm. Two points 100 nm apart cannot be conventionally resolved under\nthese model conditions. With pressure difference, viscosity and length\nfixed, doubling radius increases laminar volume flow by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^4=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=100\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, the characteristic diffusion distance is 20 µm. These are model\ncalculations, not measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the Rayleigh model predict if wavelength is halved at\nconstant aperture?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does tube flow change if radius is halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor does characteristic diffusion distance increase when\ntime quadruples?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The minimum resolvable separation halves.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof its original value.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By a factor of two, because distance is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>t<\/mi><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Resolution criteria differ by imaging modality; scattering,\naberrations and contrast affect real images. Blood vessels are not\nrigid; blood is not Newtonian under all conditions. Diffusion in\nheterogeneous tissues can depart from the simple model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Physical models quantify measurement and transport limitations. M3-L5\nconsiders uncertainty in such measurements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 4.1 Studying Cells<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Microscopy and resolution · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-7-viscosity-and-turbulence\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 1, 14.7 Viscosity and Turbulence<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLaminar flow and Poiseuille model · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics/pages/12-7-molecular-transport-phenomena-diffusion-osmosis-and-related-processes\">OpenStax\n– College Physics, 12.7 Molecular Transport Phenomena<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nRoot-mean-square diffusion distance · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-5-circular-apertures-and-resolution\">OpenStax\n– University Physics Volume 3, 4.5 Circular Apertures and Resolution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Rayleigh criterion and numerical aperture · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Measurement and uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>: variability; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>: concentration.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish calibration,\nsystematic deviation and standard uncertainty and propagate independent\nuncertainties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>An instrument can repeatedly display almost the same value yet\nmeasure systematically incorrectly. Biological interpretation requires a\ndescription of measurement quality as well as the value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"measurement-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement model<\/h3>\n<p>The measurand is the intended physical quantity, such as leaf area in\ncm². Calibration establishes the relationship between indications and\nreference values; it is not automatically adjustment. Precision\ndescribes agreement between repeated measurements. Systematic deviations\ncan arise, for example, from an incorrect zero correction. Known\nrelevant deviations are corrected; remaining incomplete knowledge\ncontributes to uncertainty.<\/p>\n<p>Standard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave the unit of the quantity and correspond to a standard deviation in\nthe uncertainty model. Type A is evaluated statistically from\nobservations; Type B is evaluated from other information such as\ncalibration certificates. This is not a classification into random and\nsystematic errors. For independent inputs and sufficiently linear\nbehaviour,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c^2(y)\\approx\\sum_i(\\partial f/\\partial x_i)^2u^2(x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCorrelation requires covariance terms. For a quotient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=n/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nindependent uncertainties give approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(c)/|c|=\\sqrt{(u(n)/n)^2+(u(V)/V)^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_016_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two independent relative uncertainties of 1 percent each combine to 1.414 percent.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two independent relative uncertainties of\n1 percent each combine to 1.414 percent.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two independent relative uncertainties of 1 percent each\ncombine to 1.414 percent.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional values:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2.00\\pm0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmmol and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50.0<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=50.0\\pm0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmL; the stated uncertainties are standard uncertainties.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=40.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmmol/L. Both relative contributions are 1%. Combined, they give 1.414%\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.57<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(c)\\approx0.57<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmmol/L. Report the result as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>40.0<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(40.0\\pm0.6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmmol/L with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not automatically a 95% interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two independent length measurements with standard uncertainties of\n0.3 and 0.4 mm are added. What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What remains in the presence of a constant zero error despite many\nrepetitions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can independence be automatically assumed with a common calibration\nstandard?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>0.3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>0.4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{0.3^2+0.4^2}=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The systematic offset; averaging does not remove it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; uncertainties may be correlated and the measurement model must\naccount for this.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Linear propagation may be unsuitable for large uncertainties or\nhighly nonlinear relationships. Biological variability between\nindividuals and instrumental measurement uncertainty must be modelled\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Measurement uncertainty makes results comparable and limits\nexaggerated precision claims. M3-L6 applies concentration and\nequilibrium models to buffers.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-5-combined-standard-uncertainty\">NIST\nTN 1297, 5. Combined Standard Uncertainty<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Propagation of standard\nuncertainties · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M3-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M3-L6 – Acid–base equilibria and buffers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Chemical and Physical Foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>: concentration; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a>: equilibrium.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain acid–base equilibria,\ncalculate buffer pH and distinguish buffer capacity from pH.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Proteins and membranes respond to changes in proton activity. Cells\ntherefore possess buffer systems and active regulatory mechanisms that\nmaintain pH within suitable limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"proton-transfer-and-equilibrium\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Proton transfer and\nequilibrium<\/h3>\n<p>In the Brønsted model, an acid donates a proton and a base accepts\none. A weak acid follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>O<\/mi><mo>⇌<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>O<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">HA+H_2O\\rightleftharpoons H_3O^++A^-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe acid dissociation constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes equilibrium;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pK_a=-\\log_{10}K_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\npH is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log_{10}a_{H^+}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot simply proton concentration. Concentrations are approximations in\ndilute solutions. At 25 °C pure water has a neutral pH of approximately\n7; neutrality means equal hydronium and hydroxide activities, not\nexactly pH 7 at every temperature.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"buffers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Buffers<\/h3>\n<p>A buffer contains a conjugate acid–base pair. Added protons are\npartly bound by the base; added base partly consumes the acid. The\nequilibrium relationship gives the approximate Henderson–Hasselbalch\nequation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pH=pK_a+\\log_{10}([A^-]/[HA])<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEqual concentrations give pH equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pK_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ratio approximately determines pH; total amount influences buffer\ncapacity. Phosphate and protein groups contribute to biological buffers;\nopen physiological systems additionally involve transport and\nregulation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_017_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Henderson–Hasselbalch model: pH versus logarithmic A−/HA ratio at pKa 6.8.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Henderson–Hasselbalch model: pH versus\nlogarithmic A−/HA ratio at pKa 6.8.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Henderson–Hasselbalch model: pH versus logarithmic A−/HA\nratio at pKa 6.8.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In a fictional closed buffer model, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pK_a=6.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand initial amounts be 10 mmol each of HA and A−. A theoretical addition\nof 2 mmol strong acid shifts the amounts to 12 mmol HA and 8 mmol A−\nunder simplified complete neutralization. At a common final volume,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.62<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pH=6.8+\\log_{10}(8/12)\\approx6.62<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a theoretical calculation, not a laboratory protocol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What pH results from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">pK_a=6.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand ratio A−/HA = 10?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two buffers have the same ratio but different total amounts in equal\nvolumes. Which has greater capacity in the model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a buffer not neutralize an arbitrarily large amount of\nacid?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">pH = 7.8.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The buffer with the greater total amount, all else equal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The conjugate base is consumed; the ratio changes strongly once its\nreserve is exhausted.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The approximation requires suitable equilibrium, known activity\nratios and sufficient quantities of both partners. Temperature, ionic\nstrength and coupled equilibria affect pH. Physiological regulation\ncannot be fully described by a closed single-buffer model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The chemical and physical foundations of M3 prepare for membrane\ntransport, metabolism and cellular regulation in M4–M6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-2-water\">OpenStax –\nBiology 2e, 2.2 Water<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Polarity, hydrogen bonding, solvent\nproperties · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers\">OpenStax\n– Chemistry 2e, 14.6 Buffers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conjugate acid/base,\nHenderson–Hasselbalch equation, capacity · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-2-equilibrium-constants\">OpenStax\n– Chemistry 2e, 13.2 Equilibrium Constants<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Equilibrium constant\nand reaction quotient · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
